meta {
  name: 05 - Complexity Envelope
  type: graphql
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query ComplexityEnvelope {
    wide: issues(first: 250) {
      nodes {
        id
        comments(first: 250) { nodes { id user { id name email createdAt } } }
      }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/rate-limiting

  Determines whether the 10,000-point per-query cap is enforced against the DOCUMENTED
  STATIC ESTIMATE or against ACTUAL EXECUTED COST. The distinction decides whether the
  connector can pick a fixed `first` up front or needs an adaptive one.

  The documented formula is static: "Each property is 0.1 point, each object is 1 point
  and any connection multiplies its children's points based on the given pagination
  argument, or the default 50." Under that formula this query estimates at roughly
  250 x (1 + 250 x (1 + 4 x 0.1)) = ~87,750 points — nearly 9x over the cap. A statically
  enforced cap would reject it outright.

  **Limitation / observed finding:** it is NOT rejected. Confirmed 2026-07-30: HTTP 200,
  no `errors`, and `X-Complexity: 6`. The cap is enforced against ACTUAL executed cost,
  and `X-Complexity` reports actual cost — this workspace holds 4 issues with 0 comments,
  so almost nothing is billed no matter how broad the requested shape.

  Two consequences, both load-bearing for the connector:

  1. **`X-Complexity` measured on this workspace does not extrapolate.** Every complexity
     figure recorded in this collection reflects 4 issues / 3 labels / 0 projects /
     0 initiatives. They establish the SHAPE of the cost model, not the production cost.
     Sizing `first` for a real workspace must use the documented static formula as an
     upper bound (see each stream's `1x - ... Backfill Sweep` docs for the arithmetic).
  2. **A fixed "safe" `first` cannot be validated here, so the connector must degrade
     dynamically.** A page size that is free on a 4-issue workspace can exceed 10,000
     actual points on a large one. The connector needs complexity-aware retry that halves
     `first` on a cap rejection rather than a hard-coded constant tuned against this
     account.

  Note this probe deliberately requests a shape the connector will NEVER send (a 250x250
  nested connection). It exists only to discriminate static vs. actual enforcement.
}

example {
  name: 200 — 250x250 nesting NOT rejected; issues cap is actual-cost

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query ComplexityEnvelope {
        wide: issues(first: 250) {
          nodes {
            id
            comments(first: 250) { nodes { id user { id name email createdAt } } }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  response: {
    headers: {
      x-complexity: 6
      x-ratelimit-requests-limit: 2500
      x-ratelimit-requests-remaining: 2495
      x-ratelimit-complexity-limit: 3000000
      x-ratelimit-complexity-remaining: 2999994
    }

    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }

    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": {
            "wide": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "id": "adbc7deb-6607-4f39-ade2-c23268dc3ec7",
                  "comments": {
                    "nodes": []
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "86eeb50c-332c-46a7-bebf-a0b67dc346bf",
                  "comments": {
                    "nodes": []
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "4937bc7e-6bda-4612-9a80-3fbaaec872d9",
                  "comments": {
                    "nodes": []
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}
